  "response": "The High-z Supernova Search Team published evidence in 1998 that distant Type Ia supernovae were fainter than expected, a conclusion resting on careful photometry of a few dozen explosions spread over a wide range of redshift. Because the Phillips relation links the decline rate of a Type Ia light curve to its peak luminosity, it indicates that such supernovae function as standardizable candles once light-curve shape has been corrected for. The 1998 measurements of excess faintness therefore show that the dimming of the distant supernovae arises from accelerating cosmic expansion. Caution was nonetheless warranted about what the photometry alone secured: either that acceleration is responsible, or the dimming arises in part from extinction by dust, with at least one of the two necessarily operating and conceivably both at once.\n\nOrdinary interstellar dust reddens the light that it dims, imposing a pronounced wavelength dependence on the absorption it produces. Precisely because reddening accompanies absorption of this kind, both teams measured supernova colors to place limits on dust reddening along the line of sight, recovering rest-frame color information from observations in several photometric bands and comparing the colors of high-redshift events with those of well-studied nearby ones. The procedure converted a question about unseen absorbing material into a tractable comparison of apparent colors.\n\nGray dust was proposed as a hypothetical absorber that would dim supernovae without reddening them, a grain population weighted toward large particles and therefore nearly achromatic across optical wavelengths. Gray dust reddens supernova light more strongly than ordinary interstellar dust does. These two characterizations of the same grain population cannot both be true, for an absorber defined by the absence of a color signature cannot simultaneously impose a color signature exceeding that of ordinary grains.\n\nSupernova 1997ff at redshift about 1.7 appeared brighter than gray-dust dimming models predicted, as a pervasive screen of absorbing grains should render the most remote objects progressively more opaque. Supernova 1997ff at redshift about 1.7 appeared fainter than any acceleration model allowed. The two readings of that single object's flux cannot be squared with each other, and one of them must be abandoned. The photometry of that exceptionally distant event was obtained before observations of supernovae beyond redshift one revealed an earlier epoch of decelerating expansion, a reversal in the expansion history that no intervening screen of absorbing material can imitate.\n\nEither the 2011 Nobel Prize in Physics was awarded to Saul Perlmutter, Brian Schmidt, and Adam Riess, or no Nobel Prize in Physics has been awarded for the discovery of cosmic acceleration; exactly one of these two states of affairs obtains, since they cannot both hold and cannot both fail. The institutional record deserves precise statement, because the honors attached to a cosmological result are frequently reported in loose terms, and the standing of the acceleration measurement within the discipline is often inferred from them.",
